WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.220
In a strategy game you hold gold and gems.

2
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.618
Each turn, every gold coin stays and earns

3
00:00:09.697 --> 00:00:11.831
two more gold and two gems

4
00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.202
every gem stays and earns one

5
00:00:14.281 --> 00:00:16.020
gold and one more gem.

6
00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:20.929
That is a matrix: three, one, two, two.

7
00:00:20.929 --> 00:00:23.968
Start with one gold coin.

8
00:00:23.968 --> 00:00:28.410
After one turn: three gold, two gems.

9
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.551
Then eleven and ten

10
00:00:30.551 --> 00:00:32.970
forty-three and forty-two

11
00:00:33.059 --> 00:00:35.477
one hundred seventy-one and

12
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:37.358
one hundred seventy.

13
00:00:37.448 --> 00:00:39.777
Every turn multiplies your

14
00:00:39.866 --> 00:00:41.747
wealth by about four

15
00:00:41.747 --> 00:00:45.240
and gold and gems become almost equal.

16
00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:48.566
Why four, and why one to one?

17
00:00:48.668 --> 00:00:51.541
And what will you hold after

18
00:00:51.644 --> 00:00:53.286
a hundred turns?

19
00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:55.030
Pause and think.

20
00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.619
Both answers are eigenvalue facts.

21
00:01:01.619 --> 00:01:05.410
The trace is five and the determinant four

22
00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.650
so lambda is four or one.

23
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.650
Four belongs to one, one

24
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:15.102
equal gold and gems grow four times over.

25
00:01:15.102 --> 00:01:18.812
One belongs to one, minus two, or

26
00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:22.416
on the same line, minus one, two

27
00:01:22.416 --> 00:01:24.960
that mix never changes.

28
00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:28.623
Now play one turn with both vectors.

29
00:01:28.623 --> 00:01:32.029
One, one is stretched to four, four.

30
00:01:32.029 --> 00:01:34.790
Minus one, two does not move.

31
00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.403
Here is the key picture.

32
00:01:37.487 --> 00:01:39.774
Use the eigenvectors as the

33
00:01:39.859 --> 00:01:41.468
axes of a new grid.

34
00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:44.178
Put them into the columns of X

35
00:01:44.178 --> 00:01:47.566
and the eigenvalues, in the same order

36
00:01:47.566 --> 00:01:50.530
on the diagonal of capital lambda.

37
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.345
In that grid, A does nothing but stretch

38
00:01:54.345 --> 00:01:57.134
four times along the first axis

39
00:01:57.134 --> 00:01:59.584
not at all along the second.

40
00:01:59.584 --> 00:02:02.880
Column by column, A X equals X lambda.

41
00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:06.535
Now take one turn in three moves.

42
00:02:06.535 --> 00:02:08.337
First, X inverse

43
00:02:08.337 --> 00:02:12.744
read the vector in eigenvector coordinates.

44
00:02:12.744 --> 00:02:16.950
The skewed axes become the ordinary ones.

45
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.911
Second, lambda

46
00:02:18.911 --> 00:02:22.347
stretch the first axis by four.

47
00:02:22.347 --> 00:02:26.213
Third, X: go back to gold and gems.

48
00:02:26.213 --> 00:02:30.830
The start, one, zero, lands on three, two

49
00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.300
exactly one turn of A.

50
00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:37.154
A equals X lambda X inverse.

51
00:02:37.154 --> 00:02:42.410
Change coordinates, stretch, change back.

52
00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.992
Now a hundred turns.

53
00:02:44.992 --> 00:02:48.295
A squared is X lambda X inverse

54
00:02:48.295 --> 00:02:50.297
X lambda X inverse

55
00:02:50.297 --> 00:02:53.500
the middle X inverse X cancels.

56
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.204
So A to the k is X, lambda to the k

57
00:02:57.204 --> 00:02:58.305
X inverse

58
00:02:58.305 --> 00:03:02.609
and lambda to the k is just four to the k

59
00:03:02.609 --> 00:03:03.510
and one.

60
00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.418
Split the start along the axes

61
00:03:06.418 --> 00:03:08.150
two thirds of x one

62
00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.295
minus one third of x two.

63
00:03:10.295 --> 00:03:11.780
To see the shape

64
00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.503
divide by four after every turn.

65
00:03:14.503 --> 00:03:16.813
The first part holds still

66
00:03:16.813 --> 00:03:20.030
the second shrinks by four, and again

67
00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:23.660
so the arrow swings onto the line of x one.

68
00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.337
Gold after k turns is two

69
00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.339
times four to the k, plus one, over three.

70
00:03:30.339 --> 00:03:32.200
After twenty turns

71
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.737
more than seven hundred billion gold

72
00:03:35.737 --> 00:03:38.250
and exactly one gem fewer.

73
00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:42.968
Here is a tempting shortcut: to square A

74
00:03:42.968 --> 00:03:45.390
just square each entry?

75
00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:49.404
That gives nine, one, four, four.

76
00:03:49.404 --> 00:03:53.878
The true square is eleven, five, ten, six.

77
00:03:53.878 --> 00:03:56.271
Test them on one, one

78
00:03:56.271 --> 00:04:00.849
which A squared must stretch sixteen times

79
00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:02.930
along its own line.

80
00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.351
The squared entries throw it off the line.

81
00:04:07.448 --> 00:04:09.871
Only a diagonal matrix is

82
00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:12.197
powered entry by entry

83
00:04:12.197 --> 00:04:16.074
which is why we power lambda and not A.

84
00:04:16.074 --> 00:04:17.043
In numpy

85
00:04:17.043 --> 00:04:20.629
A star star two squares the entries

86
00:04:20.629 --> 00:04:23.440
A at A is the matrix square.

87
00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:25.407
One more trap.

88
00:04:25.506 --> 00:04:29.173
The eigenvalue in place one of lambda

89
00:04:29.272 --> 00:04:32.344
must belong to column one of X.

90
00:04:32.344 --> 00:04:33.136
Keep X

91
00:04:33.136 --> 00:04:37.100
but write the four in the second place.

92
00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.652
Into eigen coordinates

93
00:04:39.652 --> 00:04:43.386
stretch the wrong axis, and come back.

94
00:04:43.386 --> 00:04:47.216
Now the gem-heavy mix grows four times

95
00:04:47.216 --> 00:04:49.514
and one, one stays put.

96
00:04:49.514 --> 00:04:53.727
That is a different matrix: two, minus one

97
00:04:53.727 --> 00:04:55.450
minus two, three.

98
00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:59.716
So, to sum up: the eigenvectors give the

99
00:04:59.809 --> 00:05:02.020
right grid for a matrix.

100
00:05:02.113 --> 00:05:04.877
In that grid it only stretches

101
00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:07.550
each axis by its eigenvalue.

102
00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:11.500
That is A equals X lambda X inverse

103
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.500
and powers of A become powers

104
00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.500
of the eigenvalues.

105
00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.700
The largest one wins.

106
00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:20.207
In part two

107
00:05:20.207 --> 00:05:23.143
matrices without enough eigenvectors

108
00:05:23.143 --> 00:05:26.079
and three places where powers matter

109
00:05:26.079 --> 00:05:28.629
a number sequence, a population

110
00:05:28.629 --> 00:05:30.560
and a recurrent network.
